WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.419 --> 00:00:05.922
This story is about the longest, biggest

00:00:05.922 --> 00:00:09.217
and probably most lucrative bankruptcy
in history.

00:00:09.926 --> 00:00:12.220
Lehman Brothers
staff arrived at work in London

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:15.140
this morning to be told
the bank had been put into administration.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.894
It's got $600 billion in assets.

00:00:19.894 --> 00:00:22.522
Nothing like that had ever gone
bust before.

00:00:22.522 --> 00:00:24.983
Nothing really like
that has ever gone by since.

00:00:24.983 --> 00:00:28.028
It's not good. It sucks.

00:00:28.028 --> 00:00:32.657
Everyone remembers
the boxes being carried out by bankers,

00:00:32.657 --> 00:00:34.075
but the story didn't end there.

00:00:34.075 --> 00:00:38.288
For some people,
it's continued to be their life's work.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:43.501
 I kind of was embarrassed to say
I worked at Lehman, whereas now

00:00:43.501 --> 00:00:44.836
I feel proud.

00:00:44.836 --> 00:00:49.716
This company, which is sort of, you know,
the biggest corporate failure in history.

00:00:49.716 --> 00:00:52.510
It turns out that there actually
is quite a lot of money in there.

00:00:54.137 --> 00:00:58.850
If you bought early, it's pretty hard
not to have made money on Lehman.

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:02.979
You could be looking at
maybe 40 times back your money.

00:01:02.979 --> 00:01:06.900
Having seen the value of these things
go from

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:10.612
as close to zero as anyone can be bothered
to make a trade happen at,

00:01:10.612 --> 00:01:15.909
all the way up to the highest trade
at 50%

00:01:15.909 --> 00:01:18.870
has been, of course, life changing. 

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:24.084
It's probably one of the biggest distressed
debt trades in hedge fund history.

00:01:32.509 --> 00:01:35.261
Lehman Brothers staff
arrived at work in London this morning

00:01:35.261 --> 00:01:37.972
to be told the bank had been put into
administration.

00:01:37.972 --> 00:01:40.225
 It is disastrous but I think people are experienced

00:01:40.225 --> 00:01:42.519
enough and professional enough
to take it on the chin.

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:48.233
When the news came on that Monday,
it was shock.

00:01:48.233 --> 00:01:52.654
There were just people
standing around in huddles in groups.

00:01:52.654 --> 00:01:55.490
Because we didn't know
what was going to happen.

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:57.742
You know, text messages
were flying around to say

00:01:57.742 --> 00:02:01.037
the office was going to be locked.
That we wouldn't be able to get in.

00:02:01.037 --> 00:02:05.208
That our personal belongings
would all be carted off into some skip

00:02:05.208 --> 00:02:08.336
and dumped to the side of the road.

00:02:08.336 --> 00:02:11.089
Brought down by bad morgage investments, Lehman, 

00:02:11.089 --> 00:02:15.009
which has 25,000 employees, will be liquidated.

00:02:15.009 --> 00:02:18.221
When I started working on Lehman,
one of the things I found out

00:02:18.221 --> 00:02:22.559
is that there were people working at
Lehman's in the UK and in the US as well,

00:02:22.559 --> 00:02:26.396
who had been working at Lehman's before
it went bust and they were still there.

00:02:28.064 --> 00:02:28.773
To most people,

00:02:28.773 --> 00:02:32.694
Lehman's has been long dead,
but to these people,

00:02:32.694 --> 00:02:36.281
Lehman's is still very much
a core part of their lives.

00:02:37.073 --> 00:02:41.911
So I started at Lehman April 1989,
so that's nearly 33 years ago.

00:02:42.704 --> 00:02:44.706
I think at the time
we went into insolvency,

00:02:44.706 --> 00:02:49.586
there was probably
about 6000 people in Europe.

00:02:49.586 --> 00:02:52.338
5000 plus of those were in the London

00:02:52.338 --> 00:02:53.840
headquarters.

00:02:55.717 --> 00:02:56.885
Lehman Brothers

00:02:56.885 --> 00:03:00.096
goes bust very quickly,
which is kind of unusual.

00:03:00.096 --> 00:03:04.267
Lehman was about as close
to like a freefall bankruptcy as experts

00:03:04.267 --> 00:03:07.562
would call it, than any other bankruptcy.

00:03:07.562 --> 00:03:11.566
With panic spreading, Wall Street looked for
who would be the next to fall.

00:03:11.566 --> 00:03:15.195
Number one on the list
was Lehman Brothers, with massive exposure

00:03:15.195 --> 00:03:18.364
to the crumbling mortgage market
after years of buying in big.

00:03:19.782 --> 00:03:22.368
You know, the broad story about Lehman
is that basically

00:03:22.368 --> 00:03:25.455
they got stuck with a load of subprime,

00:03:25.455 --> 00:03:30.960
subprime mortgage bonds, which are mortgage bonds
of a lower credit quality, i.e.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:33.338
the borrower is less likely to pay back

00:03:33.338 --> 00:03:36.674
than other mortgage bonds
and they can't shift them.

00:03:36.674 --> 00:03:41.304
And that makes people wonder
about the quality of their book of loans.

00:03:41.304 --> 00:03:43.389
Their assets.

00:03:43.389 --> 00:03:47.143
And because of that, the assets

00:03:47.143 --> 00:03:50.438
that Lehman
has are being sold for basically nothing,

00:03:52.106 --> 00:03:53.733
Its stock price tanking.

00:03:53.733 --> 00:03:57.779
Lehman, with the government's
help, tried to find a buyer, but no luck.

00:03:57.779 --> 00:04:01.241
After a summer of rescues,
the political appetite for another huge

00:04:01.241 --> 00:04:05.745
Wall Street bailout was gone
and the 150 year old firm was finished.

00:04:06.537 --> 00:04:09.165
Lehman's London office had to be closed
immediately.

00:04:10.875 --> 00:04:15.296
I had an email from one of my contacts,

00:04:15.296 --> 00:04:17.715
PricewaterhouseCoopers, PWC,

00:04:18.258 --> 00:04:20.802
and they said,
you know, don't freak out, but

00:04:20.802 --> 00:04:24.555
there's some guys from the insolvency team
going to be coming to the office and

00:04:24.555 --> 00:04:28.309
then once I got that email, I thought,
'Oh my God, this is serious'.

00:04:31.562 --> 00:04:33.147
In the UK

00:04:33.147 --> 00:04:37.277
PWC come in
as the administrator.

00:04:37.277 --> 00:04:42.782
Administrators basically a term for the accountants
in this case they are officers of the court

00:04:42.782 --> 00:04:46.869
who are there to look after
and wind down the business.

00:04:46.869 --> 00:04:50.373
In this morning's top corporate news,
creditors of Lehman Brothers filed

00:04:50.373 --> 00:04:54.085
more than 16,000 claims before yesterday's
deadline.

00:04:54.085 --> 00:04:57.755
Wilmington Trust, which is a trustee
for various Lehman senior notes,

00:04:57.755 --> 00:05:00.216
says it may be owed as much as 73 billion.

00:05:01.009 --> 00:05:03.177
So in the US, the UK

00:05:03.177 --> 00:05:03.594
you have

00:05:03.594 --> 00:05:07.473
these assets and you think they're going
to be worth something at some point

00:05:07.473 --> 00:05:10.935
but while everyone's panicking,
they're not going to be worth anything.

00:05:10.935 --> 00:05:12.353
Who wants to own a Lehman bond?

00:05:12.353 --> 00:05:12.770
You know,

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:16.190
you've just watched the news where
everyone's coming out with their boxes.

00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:18.026
It doesn't look like a great credit.

00:05:18.026 --> 00:05:21.112
So nobody really wants to buy it.

00:05:21.112 --> 00:05:23.740
And then you've got the other problem
that nobody has any money anyway.

00:05:23.781 --> 00:05:26.326
There's an absence of liquidity
in the financial system.

00:05:26.326 --> 00:05:29.203
The oldest money market
fund in the nation wrote off

00:05:29.203 --> 00:05:32.582
roughly three quarters
of a billion dollars in bad debt

00:05:32.582 --> 00:05:35.043
issued by the now bankrupt
Lehman Brothers.

00:05:41.841 --> 00:05:45.970
So my name is Daryl Rattigan,
I starter at Lehman in 2004 as a secondee,

00:05:45.970 --> 00:05:49.766
and I have continued to work
for the administration

00:05:49.766 --> 00:05:54.145
to manage the commercial property
related exposures that Lehman has.

00:05:55.438 --> 00:05:56.272
Lehman Brothers

00:05:56.272 --> 00:05:57.815
now go to court

00:05:57.815 --> 00:06:01.277
where a judge will take control
of the bank until its assets are sold.

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:05.823
The Lehman insolvency has various
different strands.

00:06:05.823 --> 00:06:08.576
In the UK
we have an administration process

00:06:08.576 --> 00:06:13.414
and the largest company in the UK
was a company called Lehman Brothers International Europe

00:06:13.414 --> 00:06:15.166
often referred to as LBIE

00:06:17.794 --> 00:06:20.755
Everyone likes to tidy up
after themselves.

00:06:20.755 --> 00:06:22.673
You know, when the Lehman thing fell off

00:06:22.673 --> 00:06:24.675
the cliff, there was a lot of mess
on the floor to clean up.

00:06:25.927 --> 00:06:28.596
So, if a company is insolvent,

00:06:28.596 --> 00:06:31.516
the chances are that you're
not going to get all your money back.

00:06:31.516 --> 00:06:34.894
So you'll be getting something
less than 100p in the pound.

00:06:34.894 --> 00:06:37.605
To the extent there has been something 
to sell

00:06:37.605 --> 00:06:41.442
 you might get 20, 30, 40,
50p in the pound.

00:06:41.442 --> 00:06:46.364
The LBIE creditors have actually got
something around about 140p in the pound. 

00:06:47.323 --> 00:06:50.284
So when a company goes bust,

00:06:50.284 --> 00:06:54.497
it has to pay its creditors
and it has to do it in a particular order.

00:06:54.497 --> 00:06:58.126
And that queue we call a waterfall.

00:06:58.126 --> 00:07:02.130
Basically what happens is
the cash comes out of the company

00:07:02.130 --> 00:07:06.008
and then it has to go to someone first
and then they'll go to a second person

00:07:06.008 --> 00:07:07.385
and then it'll go to a third person.

00:07:07.385 --> 00:07:11.722
So imagine a waterfall coming down
and you've got these little buckets

00:07:11.722 --> 00:07:15.518
and the top bucket
might be called Super Senior Secured,

00:07:15.518 --> 00:07:18.855
and then there might be a bucket
in the middle called Unsecured,

00:07:18.855 --> 00:07:21.691
and then there'll be another bucket
called Subordinated and then there'll be

00:07:21.691 --> 00:07:25.486
another bucket called Equity.
And people get paid in that order.

00:07:25.486 --> 00:07:27.613
So once the first bucket is full up,

00:07:27.613 --> 00:07:30.908
then the next bucket gets filled up,
then the next bucket gets filled up

00:07:31.784 --> 00:07:34.579
and those buckets
have been filled up a lot.

00:07:35.496 --> 00:07:38.249
If you're looking at the European

00:07:38.249 --> 00:07:41.961
UK listed, UK issue debt,

00:07:41.961 --> 00:07:45.423
you will get paid 100% of the face value.

00:07:45.423 --> 00:07:49.135
Bare in mind these are being sold
for nothing close to that value

00:07:49.135 --> 00:07:53.347
when the company was going bust and
then you get a load of interest on top.

00:07:53.347 --> 00:07:55.683
And in the UK.
some of the people who held on

00:07:55.683 --> 00:07:58.769
and waited for that bucket
to fill up, have had to wait a long time

00:07:58.769 --> 00:08:01.063
but they've got their buckets overflowing.

00:08:05.943 --> 00:08:06.944
Hi, I'm Robert Southey.

00:08:06.944 --> 00:08:08.905
I'm the founder of Southey Capital.

00:08:08.905 --> 00:08:13.201
And we are a firm that are focused on trading
bankruptcy claims and distressed debt.

00:08:13.659 --> 00:08:17.163
So the LBIE debts,

00:08:17.163 --> 00:08:21.667
they finally settled at about 144% of par.

00:08:21.667 --> 00:08:25.338
So the holders of that
who held all the way through received

00:08:25.338 --> 00:08:28.549
a substantial recovery
plus statutory interest.

00:08:29.967 --> 00:08:32.053
And they the whole estate

00:08:32.053 --> 00:08:35.598
probably paid out around 17 billion
sterling.

00:08:36.766 --> 00:08:39.060
The vast majority of the recovery

00:08:39.060 --> 00:08:41.729
is coming from assets which were marked

00:08:41.729 --> 00:08:44.815
at a very low value in 2008

00:08:44.815 --> 00:08:48.444
and subsequently recovered huge amounts.

00:08:48.444 --> 00:08:52.615
And had enough time
been given to the company, perhaps,

00:08:52.615 --> 00:08:57.119
it would have traded out
of its problems and survived.

00:08:58.579 --> 00:09:00.957
Much more money has been realized

00:09:00.957 --> 00:09:04.752
from Lehman's estate
than would have been thought possible.

00:09:04.752 --> 00:09:07.463
It's pretty unusual that unsecured

00:09:07.463 --> 00:09:11.008
creditors would get paid this much money.

00:09:11.008 --> 00:09:14.220
Pretty unusual
that they get paid back in full.

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:16.347
Very unusual that they get interest.

00:09:16.347 --> 00:09:18.516
And in between that,
you actually have the taxman.

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:21.602
So a billion pounds of tax was

00:09:21.602 --> 00:09:26.190
paid to HMRC, which, you know,
probably isn't in the press very much

00:09:26.190 --> 00:09:30.152
but, you know, that's a big
number for the Exchequer to receive.

00:09:31.112 --> 00:09:34.365
So now I kind of feel proud and
when people say, 'Oh, who'd you work for?'

00:09:34.365 --> 00:09:37.994
and I say, 'Lehman'. They, 'ah, Lehman
is that still going?

00:09:37.994 --> 00:09:39.245
We thought that went ages ago'.

00:09:39.245 --> 00:09:40.997
And you say, 'Well, actually,
no, that was six

00:09:40.997 --> 00:09:44.875
and a half thousand people in Europe,
and now there's four or five of us left'

00:09:44.875 --> 00:09:47.795
and they just cannot believe it.

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:50.840
I'm not sure I want to be the last person
to turn lights off,

00:09:50.840 --> 00:09:55.094
but I'm kind of quite keen to be

00:09:55.094 --> 00:09:58.764
around to close those last
remaining entities.

00:09:58.764 --> 00:09:59.515
That's it.

00:09:59.515 --> 00:10:01.934
Blank piece of paper
again and I can move on.

